---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <Favicons />
    <title>{metaTitle}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={metaDescription} />
    <link rel="canonical" href="/seo/backlinks/entrepreneurship-posting-sites" />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={metaTitle} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={metaDescription} />
    <JsonLd graph={graph} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <section class="page-header pillar-hero" style="--topic: rgba(241,94,74,0.16); --topic-fg: #d8412c;">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="breadcrumbs">
            <a href="/">Home</a><span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo">SEO</a><span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo/backlinks">Link Building</a><span>&#8250;</span>
            <span>Entrepreneurship Posting Sites</span>
          </div>
          <span class="card-icon pillar-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-rocket" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Link building</p>
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Entrepreneurship publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on whether you have built something or just written about building.</p>
            <p>The major business media have narrowed their contributor programmes. The startup outlets take founder journeys with numbers. The small-business resources take practical how-tos. The community blogs are open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Major business media</td><td>Entrepreneur, Inc, Forbes, Fast Company, Business Insider, Harvard Business Review</td><td>Not open guest posts. A distinctive operator story, exclusive data, or expert commentary</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Startup &amp; founder outlets</td><td>TechCrunch, Startup Grind, Foundr, StartupNation, YourStory, Tech in Asia</td><td>Founder journeys with real numbers, fundraising breakdowns, market analysis</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Small-business resources</td><td>SBA.gov, SCORE, Business News Daily, Small Biz Trends, AllBusiness, Grasshopper</td><td>Practical how-tos for owners, operations, hiring, cash flow, marketing</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Founder &amp; community blogs</td><td>Addicted2Success, Under30CEO, Side Hustle Nation, Startup Talky, IdeaMensch</td><td>Personal lessons, side-hustle case studies, community stories</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the "entrepreneur" niche is full of recycled motivational content. Editors want a real operator story, real numbers, or a lesson you paid for, not another "10 habits of successful people" post.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and dormant blogs were replaced with active publications. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {SITES.map((s) => (
                  <tr><td>{s.n}</td><td>{s.site}</td><td>{s.dr}</td><td>{s.da}</td><td>{s.pa}</td><td>{s.ur}</td><td>{s.traffic}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the number or the failure.</strong> "We hit $1M ARR in 14 months with a team of 3" or "the pricing mistake that cost us 40% of revenue" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Show the receipts.</strong> A revenue graph, a hiring timeline, a real P&amp;L detail.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your founder stories" or "for the small-business operations coverage" shows you read the outlet.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> A framework, a template, a spreadsheet.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> Business media want a reported story. Startup outlets want a founder journey. Resource sites want a how-to.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose the company</strong> you are writing about or from.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it exists only to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a "habits of successful entrepreneurs" listicle.</li>
              <li>Tell a success story with no numbers.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch to Forbes and a side-hustle blog.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("business plan software") on links back to your product.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated motivational content with no real operator behind it.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> one placement on Inc, Entrepreneur or a respected startup outlet, built on a real founder story with numbers, beats a dozen links from thin motivational-content sites.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>{toc.map((item) => (<li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>))}</ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq"><Faq items={faqs} /></div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>We shortlist the entrepreneurship outlets that carry weight, shape your founder story or data, write the pitches and place them. No link farms, no risk.</p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a href={whatsappUrl('Hi SERP Mentor, I would like help with entrepreneurship and startup guest posting and digital PR.')} class="button-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
